\slide{
\cowel_html_element("h1", (style="font-size: 350%")){Bit-precise integers\br
\span(id="docnum"){P3666R5}}
}

\slide{
\slideh{Introduction}
C23 now has \tcode{_BitInt} type for N-bit integers:
\cppblock{
// 8-bit unsigned integer initialized with value 255.
// The literal suffix wb is unnecessary in this case.
unsigned _BitInt(8) x = 0xFFwb;
}
\ul{
  \li{implemented by GCC (C) and Clang (C & C++); max: \tcode{_BitInt(8'388'608)}}
  \li{forwarded by EWG to LEWG/CWG for C++29}
  \li{extensive and ever-increasing support in libc++}
  \li{open GCC (C++) and libstdc++ patches to add \tcode{_BitInt} support}
}
}

\slide{
\slideh{Reminder \'EM DASH' P3666 type taxonomy}

\pre{
integral types
   ├── signed or unsigned integer types
   │   ├── standard integers: \tcode{int}, \tcode{unsigned}, etc.
   │   ├── extended integers: \tcode{__int128} etc.
   │   └── bit-precise integers: (\tcode{unsigned}) \tcode{_BitInt(N)}
   ├── character types: \tcode{char}, \tcode{wchar_t}, etc.
   └── \tcode{bool}
}

\ul{
  \li{P3666R5 mostly matches C2y taxonomy}
  \li{e.g. \q{character types} are different}
}
}

\slide{
\slideh{P3666 consensus decisions in recent history}

\ul{
  \li{\b{P3666R3}: Seen briefly in Croydon 2026\ul{
    \li{\q{We should prevent library support for \tcode{_BitInt}}}
    \li{\q{\tcode{std::is_integral_v<_BitInt(N)>} should be \tcode{false}}}
  }}
  \li{\b{P3666R4}: Discussed in Brno 2026 EWG/LEWG joint session\ul{
    \li{\q{\tcode{std::is_integral_v<_BitInt(N)>} should be \tcode{true}}}
    \li{\q{We agree with the design direction of section 5 in P3666R4}}
  }
  }
}

\style{
  .quad {
    font-size: 70%;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 5rem;
  }
}
}

\slide{
\slideh{Key question}

\style{
  #key-question {
    font-size: 175%;
    margin: 10rem 4rem 0 4rem;
    text-align: center;
  }
}

\div(id="key-question"){
Do we want to support \tcode{_BitInt(N)} \em{fully} in the standard library,
same as other signed or unsigned integer types?
}
}

\slide{
\slideh{Arguments in favor of support}

\ul{
  \li{Consistency with other integer types}
  \li{Respect for what \tcode{std::is_integral_v} implies}
  \li{Support is simply useful for math stuff}
  \li{Exposing numeric intrinsics\ul{
    \li{e.g. \tcode{std::popcount} → \tcode{__builtin_popcountg} → \tcode{@llvm.ctpop.i128}}
    \li{zero-overhead principle}
    \li{don't leave room for lower-level features}
  }}
  \li{not breaking existing libc++ support}
}
}

\slide{
\slideh{libc++ support for \tcode{_BitInt(N)}}

\style{
  #libcxx {
    font-size: 75%;
    margin-left: auto;
    margin-right: auto;
  }
  #libcxx td {
    vertical-align: top;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-color);
  }
}

\table(id="libcxx"){
  \tr{
    \th{Header}
    \th{Supported features}
  }
  \tr{
    \td{\header{limits}}
    \td{
      \tcode{std::numeric_limits<_BitInt(N)>}
    }
  }
  \tr{
    \td{\header{concepts}}
    \td{
      \tcode{std::integral<_BitInt(N)> etc.}
    }
  }
  \tr{
    \td{\header{type_traits}}
    \td{
      \tcode{std::is_integral<_BitInt(N)>}, \tcode{std::make_signed<_BitInt(N)>}, etc.
    }
  }
  \tr{
    \td{\header{utility}}
    \td{
      \tcode{std::byte{} << 0wb},
      \tcode{std::to_integer<_BitInt(N)>},
      \tcode{std::integer_sequence<_BitInt(N)>},
      \tcode{std::cmp_*},
      \tcode{std::hash<_BitInt(N)>}
    }
  }
  \tr{
    \td{\header{bit}}
    \td{
      bit manipulation functions
    }
  }
  \tr{
    \td{\header{mdspan}}
    \td{
      \tcode{std::mdspan::extents<_BitInt(N)>} etc.
    }
  }
  \tr{
    \td{\header{random}}
    \td{
      \tcode{std::seed_seq} operations
    }
  }
  \tr{
    \td{\header{numeric}}
    \td{
      \tcode{std::gcd} and \tcode{std::lcm},
      \tcode{std::saturating_*},
      \tcode{std::midpoint}
    }
  }
  \tr{
    \td{\header{charconv}}
    \td{
      \tcode{std::to_chars} and \tcode{std::from_chars}
    }
  }
  \tr{
    \td{\header{cstdlib}}
    \td{
      \tcode{std::abs}
    }
  }
  \tr{
    \td{\header{cmath}}
    \td{
      \tcode{_BitInt} is treated as \tcode{double} when used as an argument.
      \tcode{std::abs}.
    }
  }
  \tr{
    \td{\header{chrono}}
    \td{
      \tcode{_BitInt} as representation type of \tcode{std::chrono::duration}
    }
  }
}
}

\slide{
\slideh{Other support for \tcode{_BitInt(N)}}

\style{
  #c23-compat {
    margin-left: auto;
    margin-right: auto;
  }
  #c23-compat td {
    vertical-align: top;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-color);
  }
}

\table(id="c23-compat"){
  \tr{
    \th{Header}
    \th{Support}
  }
  \tr{
    \td{\header{version}}
    \td{\tcode{__cpp_lib_bitint}}
  }
  \tr{
    \td{\header{stdbit.h}}
    \td{C bit manipulation functions}
  }
  \tr{
    \td{\header{climits}}
    \td{\tcode{#define BITINT_MAXWIDTH}}
  }
}
}

\slide{
\slideh{Arguments against support}

\ul{
  \li{General implementation effort\ul{
    \li{including testing effort}
  }}
  \li{Supporting an \q{ugly} C compatibility feature}
  \li{Cannibalizing space in standard for future \q{proper C++ integers}}
}
}

\slide{
\slideh{What if we do nothing?}

\ul{
  \li{GCC and Clang will move on without us\ul{
    \li{\tcode{_BitInt(N)} keeps living as a compiler extension}
  }}
  \li{Users and implementers are deprived of a standard form of feature\ul{
    \li{Ever-increasing pressure to standardize existing practice}
  }}
  \li{Remaining C23 ABI incompatibility}
}
}
